Let's call the real value of the variable $x$, after borrowing $-10$ from it, that we'll name $y$. \begin{align*}
x+\tfrac{45}{x-4}&=–10\\
\tfrac{45}{x-4}&=–10-x.
\end{align*}
We'll add $4$ to both sides because we want the equation that results from that step, $y^2$, to be able to be factored. \begin{align*}
\dfrac{45}{x-4}&=–10-x\\
\dfrac{45}{x-4}+4&=–10-x+4\\
\dfrac{45}{x-4}+\dfrac{4}{1}&=–x-6.
\end{align*}
Now that we have a fraction, we'll top them up with the same denominator. To do that, we'll find the least common denominator, which is $(x-4)\cdot1$ or simply $x-4$. \begin{align*}
\dfrac{45}{x-4}+\dfrac{4}{1}&=–x-6\\
\dfrac{45}{x-4}+\dfrac{4(x-4)}{x-4}&=–x-6.
\end{align*}
We'll then multiply the top and bottom of $\tfrac{4(x-4)}{x-4}$ by $(x-4)$ so that we have equivalent fractions (they still have the same value) and we can put them into our equation: \begin{align*}
\dfrac{45}{x-4}+\dfrac{4(x-4)}{x-4}&=–x-6\\
\dfrac{45}{x-4}+\dfrac{4\cdot(x-4)}{(x-4)\cdot(x-4)}&=–x-6.
\end{align*}
Now that we have two fractions with a common denominator, we'll add the numerators on the left-hand-side, then factor out the $(x+10)$. \begin{align*}
\dfrac{45}{x-4}+\dfrac{4(x-4)}{(x-4)(x-4)}&=–x-6\\
\dfrac{45+(4x-16)}{(x-4)(x-4)} &=–x-6\\
\dfrac{4x-16+45}{(x-4)(x-4)} &=–(x+6).
\end{align*}
The \texttt{(} and \texttt{)} signs are useful for the curves that might appear in math problems. However we didn't need anything in this problem, so we'll take them out. \begin{align*}
\dfrac{4x-16+45}{(x-4)(x-4)} &=–(x+6)\\
\dfrac{(4x+29)}{(x-4)(x-4)} &=-(x+6).
\end{align*}
Now we'll cross-multiply. To do that, we'll multiply both the numerator on the left and the denominator on the right. To do that, we'll also multiply both the numerator on the right and the denominator on the left. \begin{align*}
\dfrac{(4x+29)}{(x-4)(x-4)}&=–(x+6)\\
(4x+29)(x-4)&=-(x+6)(x-4)(x-4)
\end{align*}\

