Of course, this is a very simple application of the Remainder Theorem.  $$\\~\\~\left\{\begin{matrix}
&p(3) = 11\\
\end{matrix}\right.\sim\left\{\begin{matrix}
&(A*3^5 + B *3^3 + C*3*1 + 4 = 11)
\end{matrix}\right.\sim\left.\begin{matrix}
&A*B=C=\frac{11}{270}
\end{matrix}\right\} \\~\\~$$
$\\~\\$
Notice that $\left.\begin{matrix}
&A*C = B*3
\end{matrix}\right\rvert 0,$ because -3 is also a root of the polynomial in lower degree.
$\\~\\$
\begin{align}
&p(-3) &= \big[\big(A(-3)^5+B(-3)^3+C(-3) + 4\big)\big]\\~\\
& =& -243 * A - 27*B -3*C*1 + 4\\~\\
& =& -270* \big[A*B+C*1\big] + 4\\~\\
&=& -270* \bigg[\big(A*B+C*1\big)\bigg(\frac{11}{270} \bigg) \bigg(3\bigg)\bigg]\bigg(\frac{11}{270} \bigg) \bigg(3\bigg)+ 4\\~\\
&=& -\bigg[I.3.\bigg] + 4\\~\\
\end{align}
\begin{align}
&=& -\bigg[I.11 + 4]\\~\\
&=& -15
\end{align}
$\\~\\$
